social media is just really recently  taking off as a marketing channel so  what we're seeing is that sort of on  surveys among CMOS they say right now  social media is about 10% of marketing  budgets in 2018 is projected to be 20%  we're seeing that increasing amounts of  marketing dollars are getting shifted  from more traditional channels towards  social media more broadly so this would  involve both advertising through social  media as well as firms trying to kind of  foster consumers to talk about their  brands by providing platforms to leave  reviews by sometimes responding to them  and there's a lot of ways in which are  trying to do it so it seemed to channel  and if you ask CMO is about 64% of GMOs  think word-of-mouth online word of mouth  is one of the most powerful marketing  channels in the future but at the same  time it's actually not that well  understood how quantitatively strong  these effects can be and that's where  our study comes in what we try to look  for is and if you will an external shock  to the system something that changed how  much people were talking about a  particular product or brand that wasn't  related to any underlying  characteristics of the brand so if we  wanted to avoid us that your talk more  people are talking about a vent because  more people bought it in the past so  essentially the talking about is a  result of the sales but but we want to  establish is that where the talking can  actually need to more future sales in  that realm and that's usually hard to do  we looked at sina weibo which is the  chinese equivalent of twitter if you  will a very similar type of platform  twitter itself is blocked in mainland  china so it cannot be used so Sina Weibo  emerged as a substitute for that and  what happened is that in 2012 I'm  following a political scandal Sina Weibo  was partially blocked for three days and  so what we're investigating is we're  going to look at TV shows a lot of them  have a very active following a lot of  them are aired at a daily level so we  see every day people talk about these  episodes and they watch it at night and  suddenly for three days a lot of this  activity is gone due to this external  influence which is the censorship event  that's coming from the government and so  essentially looking at does something  happen to show us that tended to have a  lot of following and suddenly disappears  during those three days do we see that  being reflected in their viewership  numbers but the censorship event helped  us a lot in pinning something down  that's otherwise very hard to do so most  studies prior to that usually it's  something like relating sales of a  product to the number of tweets  pertaining to the product maybe from the  previous day or week something to assure  that's not happening at the same time  but even then it's hard to know whether  that's the right attribution so you  might imagine again the example there  might be one high quality product which  constantly has a lot of sales and  constantly has a lot of tweets about it  another product does not have that then  even if we look at temporal differences  between sales and the micro-blogging  that precedes it that might still give  us just a correlation rather than actual  an impact off the micro-blogging in a  causal sense onto the sales numbers and  it's a lot of reasons to think that the  if you will more naive approaches tend  to overstate the effect so the the  incorrect measurement would tend to go  towards overstatement rather than  understatement of the effect the reason  being that probably the most salient  reason is that the actual causal  relationship could be from sales to  word-of-mouth rather than the other way  around and this might be Mis attributed  so it could be the case that you sell  the product a lot and because a lot of  people buy  they then go on social media and talk  about it so it's really the sales that  precede the word-of-mouth rather than  the other way around which is what we  want to establish now in our case we can  rule that out because the censorship  event wasn't based on sales numbers or  anything it's really external to the  system and it impacts microblogging  activity and we can then trace through  the effect of that on to sales and we  can be sure that that reduction  microblogging does not originate from  sales differences which would be the  case if we just compare different  products with each other so what we find  is that relative to the typical movement  in TV ratings are doubling the number  doubling microblogging activity moves it  by about 20 percent of the typical  movement that happens from episode to  episode which coming from just one  advertising channel in this realm is  actually a fairly significant the  findings are significant in two ways so  one is I think both intuitively for a  lot of practitioners as well as in the  prior literature academic literature we  often thought these effects might be  even larger than what we find so to the  extent that firms increasingly shifting  their marketing dollars into those  channels it's very useful to know that  maybe these effects might have been  under overstated and we have to be  careful in sort of managing them  secondly it's it's a realm where it's  just incredibly hard to study these  effects because user-generated content  isn't under your control as a firm so  you can't manipulate it in order to  experiment around with it they actually  have to resort to methods like the one  that we're using which we call quasi  experimental we're not doing the  experiment ourselves but we have  something that is almost like an  experiment the Chinese government if you  will experimented for us by shutting it  down for three days they didn't mean to  shut it down for TV shows but it so  happened that's something that we can  leverage to actually understand the  effect it's a study that cautions a  little bit against overstating that is  sort of the magic bullet in terms of  marketing channels so it doesn't seem  like it's clearly outperforming other  channels so it seems to me of anything  we still want to stick with a certain  mix  of marketing tools rather than diverting  everything into that realm
 

